\details{
This is the classic interface based on a transition matrix whose
cells name rate functions. \code{\link[=micsim_model]{micsim_model()}} provides an alternative
interface that keeps the model inputs together and accepts functions
directly. Both use the same simulation engine.

\strong{The state space} contains all possible combinations of the model's state
variables, with each state written as its values joined by forward
slashes: \code{"f/DE/1"} for a German woman with one child. The absorbing
states are the columns of \code{transitionMatrix} after the transient ones;
any label works.

\strong{Labels with a meaning.} This interface keeps three conventions of
MicSim 3.0.0, because it has no other way to be told: only states
containing \code{"f"} can give birth through \code{fertTr}; with
\code{monthSchoolEnrol}, an allowed transition counts as enrolment
if it changes one state variable from \code{"no"} to \code{"low"} and leaves
the other values as they were; and \code{\link[=buildTransitionMatrix]{buildTransitionMatrix()}} puts
\code{"dead"} and \code{"rest"} first among the absorbing states.

\strong{Rates} are functions of \code{age}, \code{calTime} and \code{duration}, in any
subset, all in fractional years. They are transition intensities, not
probabilities: a rate may exceed one. They must be vectorised, because
the simulation integrates them numerically.

\strong{Reproducibility.} \code{micSim()} takes no seed. It draws the seed of
the run from the random stream the caller has set up (one draw), so
\code{set.seed(234)} immediately before the call makes a run reproducible.
Every individual then draws from their own random-number stream, as
described in \code{\link[=simulate.micsim_model]{simulate.micsim_model()}}.
}
\section{Reproducibility across versions}{

